    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm preping a chemistry demo for my students about why cutting onions makes your eyes sting. Can you explain the specific chemical pathway from the moment the knife hits the onion to the actual irritation in the eyes? I need to explain the enzymatic part. Thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Of course! When you cut an onion, the cell walls are damaged, and the enzyme alliinase is released. Alliinase catalyzes a reaction with the amino acid sulfoxides found within the cell to form sulfides. Thiosulfates are by-products of this reaction. The final step involves the conversion of the by-products into sulfenic acids, which readily combine with the moisture present in your eyes to form sulfenic acid derivatives. These sulfenic acids dissolve easily in tissue fluids, causing chemical irritation and the sensation of tearing.",
