    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start, thanks. Could you go into a bit more detail about the propanethial S-oxide part? I want to make sure I explain why the reaction seems to happen so suddenly and sharply once the onion is sliced. Is there something about the volatility or the way the gas hits the eyes that makes it feel so aggressive?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Indeed, when you slice an onion, it releases propanethial S-oxide, which is a volatile sulfur compound. Propanethial S-oxide is converted within the onion tissue to the very irritant sulfur dioxide gas upon exposure to enzymes. Since it's gaseous, it diffuses rapidly through the air, dispersing downwards almost immediately after the cutting process. Upon reaching your eyes, sulfur dioxide reacts with the moisture in the eye to form harmful sulfurous acid aerosols that cause irritation. This sudden presence and the rapid spread of the irritants from the volatilized compounds are what make the stinging sensation so immediate and intense. Propanethial S-oxide's volatility not only contributes to its ability to diffuse quickly but also affects its potency in causing irritation. Propane, being a hydrocarbon, has a lower boiling point, meaning that it rapidly transforms from liquid to vapor upon heating or contact. Opting for safety goggles ensures minimal eye contact with these vapors, thereby mitigating the impact. The molecular interactions can be complex on a more detailed level, involving not only the compounds directly but also intricate pathways within the human body's defense against mild irritants, transforming the relatively benign chemical sensation into an aggressive, noticeable irritation.",
